{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffR7KCrMCyEREmoUJLie","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the three files and the self-check passed (`OK`). On a short smoke fit the new model's ELPD-LOO was −2300.6, against −2317.6 for the incumbent. That fit is too short to compare the two properly, but it's a promising sign.\n\n**Model name:** `tally_span_switch_triplet_variety`\n\n**Hypothesis:** This refines the incumbent `iter2_candidate3`. Everything it does is kept: people still judge a sequence by how close its heads-minus-tails tally span comes to their own expected span. They still reward switching between H and T, with the strength of that reward varying by person. And each person still has a small lean toward clicking left or right. The one change is a shared reward on local pattern variety: the share of distinct overlapping three-flip chunks a sequence contains. I took this component from `iter1_candidate4`. Strict alternation like HTHTHTHT contains only two chunks (HTH and THT), and blocks like HHTTHHTT also reuse a few chunks, so both now look patterned.\n\n**Why this change:** It targets the two critique findings that survive the false-discovery correction:\n- People pick the perfect alternator less often than the model predicts (observed 0.53 vs predicted 0.60). The incumbent's straight-line switching reward over-rewards it.\n- When two sequences switch equally often, people pick the one with more distinct three-flip chunks more often than predicted (observed 0.63 vs predicted 0.57).\n\nBecause the variety reward pulls against the switching reward only for highly regular sequences, it may also explain the third surviving finding (among pairs that both switch a lot, people pick the more-switching one more often than predicted). I haven't tested that.\n\n**Not yet established:**\n- **Novelty:** the self-check doesn't test whether this model's predictions differ enough from the incumbent's, and admission does.\n- **Convergence:** the smoke fit used one chain, so R-hat couldn't be computed and convergence wasn't checked. Admission's full fit has to pass that gate.\n- **ELPD reliability:** the smoke fit flagged the ELPD-LOO estimate as unreliable, with 8.6% of trials over the Pareto-k threshold against a 1% tolerance. A model with an unreliable estimate can't be exported as the winner, so it can't take over from the incumbent unless the full fit clears this.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run2/data/subjective_randomness/experiment2/model_loop/iter_3/candidate_4/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3423,"cache_read_input_tokens":73071,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3423,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"42f7fa2e-7705-4498-8c37-a583e96bc75d","uuid":"9e34157a-d288-4a3a-9246-06fd9bfda5b7","timestamp":"2026-10-03T13:54:19.367Z","request_id":"req_011CffR7JoJUHu25d67HVyQi"}
